# MODEL PICKS FOR A MacBook Pro M3 24 GB (base M3, 10-core GPU)
# -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# hardware: Apple M3 (4P+4E), 10-core GPU, 24 GB unified memory, ~100
# GB/s memory bandwidth. NOTE: BASE M3 — not Pro (150 GB/s) or Max
# (300-400 GB/s). bandwidth is the binding constraint on tok/s here,
# not GPU cores and not capacity.
#
# MEMORY BUDGET: macOS reserves roughly a quarter of unified memory for
# the system, so ollama can address ~18 GB (sysctl iogpu.wired_limit_mb,
# 0 = default). CONFIRMED FROM THE SERVER LOG on this machine: Metal
# reports total_vram="17.8 GiB", ollama schedules against
# available="17.3 GiB", and llama.cpp sees 18185 MiB free. so ~18 GB is
# the real number — NOT the "~2/3 of RAM" figure repeated in a lot of
# Apple Silicon writeups. that difference decides whether an 18.6 GB
# model fits, so it matters.
#
# AND WEIGHTS DON'T ALL LAND ON THE GPU: every load in the log splits
# the model, with 5-10% staying host-resident (qwen2.5-coder:14b 417 of
# 8565 MiB = 4.9%, gemma4:12b 787 of 7811 = 10.1%, gpt-oss:20b 1104 of
# 13140 = 8.4%). so the GPU only has to hold ~90-95% of the DOWNLOAD
# size. unified memory means it's the same DRAM either way — but it
# does mean the fit check is more forgiving than the download size
# suggests.
#
# the COMFORTABLE budget is weights <= ~9 GB. the old note here called
# ~13 GB a "quit the browser first" ceiling — the log does not support
# that either: gpt-oss:20b at 13.8 GB used 12234 of 18185 MiB and left
# 5951 MiB free. call ~14 GB the practical ceiling and ~17 GB the real
# one. unified memory means no PCIe transfer — but also no separate
# pool, so the model competes with everything else.
#
# SPEED RULE OF THUMB — DENSE MODELS ONLY: tok/s ~= 100 / model-size-
# in-GB, minus ~10%. an 8 GB model gives ~11 tok/s, a 5 GB model ~18
# tok/s, a 2 GB model ~45 tok/s. CALIBRATED, not guessed: gemma4:12b
# measured 11.0 tok/s and qwen2.5-coder:14b 9.8 tok/s on this machine,
# both landing at 88-90 GB/s of the ~100 GB/s nominal ceiling (~89%
# efficiency). the older "minus ~20%" here was too pessimistic.
#
# THIS RULE IS WRONG FOR MoE — off by 4x on gpt-oss:20b, which the rule
# puts at ~6 tok/s and which actually measures 22.2. MoE reads only the
# routed experts per token, so use:
#   effective_GB ~= total_GB x (active_params / total_params) x 1.7-2.2
#   tok/s        ~= 89 / effective_GB
# the 1.7-2.2 corrects for the parts that are DENSE and get read every
# token anyway — attention, embeddings, router, shared expert. IT IS A
# RANGE, NOT A CONSTANT: fitting it to the only two measurements
# available gives x1.69 (gpt-oss:20b) and x2.20 (gemma4:26b). so treat
# MoE predictions as +/-25%, and prefer the high end for models with a
# shared always-on expert. see the MoE section below.
#
# prompt processing on Apple Silicon is far slower than NVIDIA — first
# token on a long prompt feels sluggish even when generation speed is
# fine. MoE helps here too: measured prefill is ~300 tok/s on
# gpt-oss:20b vs ~98 on gemma4:12b and ~70 on qwen2.5-coder:14b.
#
# US-DOMESTIC FILTER: picks below are limited to models from US orgs
# (Google, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI, IBM, NVIDIA, AI2, Nomic). that rules
# out Qwen / DeepSeek (China), Mistral / Devstral (France), and
# mxbai-embed (Germany). the honest cost is CODE — qwen2.5-coder:7b is
# meaningfully better than anything US-domestic in the 7B class. general
# chat and reasoning stay competitive; code takes a real hit.
#
# sizes below are ollama download sizes at the default quant, read from
# the registry manifest rather than guessed:
#   curl -s https://registry.ollama.ai/v2/library/gemma4/manifests/12b \
#     | python3 -c "import json,sys;print(sum(l['size'] for l in json.load(sys.stdin)['layers'])/1e9)"

