# MAC-SPECIFIC TIPS
#   OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION=1 OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=1 OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE=24h \
#     ollama serve                     # the setup worth making permanent
#   sudo sysctl iogpu.wired_limit_mb=20480  # raise the GPU ceiling to 20 GB.
#                                      #   resets on reboot; starves the OS if
#                                      #   you overdo it. last resort only
#   ollama ps                          # what's loaded + how much memory
#   sudo powermetrics --samplers gpu_power -i 1000   # confirm GPU is busy
#   there is no GPU/CPU split to tune — unified memory means the whole
#   model is "on the GPU" the moment it loads, or it doesn't fit.
#   ON BATTERY: expect roughly half the tok/s. macOS clocks the GPU down
#   hard when unplugged, and a sustained 8 GB model will drain a 14"
#   battery in about 90 minutes.
#
# MTP (multi-token prediction) — WHY IT MATTERS ON APPLE SILICON
#   generation on M-series is bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound. every
#   token forces reading the full active-weight set from unified memory,
#   so tok/s is capped by GB/s ÷ GB-per-forward-pass. MTP breaks that
#   ceiling: a tiny draft head (~400 MB) proposes the next N tokens in
#   a single forward pass, the main model verifies them in ONE more
#   forward pass, and accepted tokens are effectively free. at 70%
#   acceptance with n_max=3 the THEORETICAL ceiling is ~2x the naive
#   tok/s; MEASURED on gemma4:26b it comes out closer to ~1.6x once
#   draft-pass and rejection overhead are paid. still the difference
#   between "watchable" and "usable" on M4 Pro.
#   Detection: `ollama ps` won't show it directly. grep the ollama serve
#   log for `common_specu` — this build prints, at every model load,
#     spec common_specu: no implementations specified for speculative decoding
#   when no draft head is present. `prompt_save` lines carry a matching
#   `(draft: 0.000 MiB)`. (an earlier note here said to grep for
#   `--spec-type`; that flag does not appear in this build's log.)
#   draft acceptance rate appears in llama.cpp verbose output as
#   "draft acceptance = X".
#   Models that ship an MTP head today: gemma4:26b (the standout), and
#   a growing list of 2026 releases. NOT a family feature — worth
#   checking per model. gemma4:26b has an MTP head; gemma4:12b, despite
#   the shared architecture name, is plain dense — CONFIRMED in the
#   Sep 2026 log, which prints "common_specu: no implementations
#   specified for speculative decoding" on the gemma4:12b load and
#   measures a flat 11.0 tok/s with no draft acceptance. (the
#   conclusion was right; the "no --spec-type flag" evidence cited
#   here originally was not what the log actually shows.) check
#   `ollama show` for
#   `draft_num_predict` — if it's set, an MTP head is bundled and active.
#   Models WITHOUT an MTP head still hit the bandwidth ceiling — no
#   ollama-level flag turns speculative decoding on retroactively; the
#   draft model has to exist in the manifest.
#   Fastest way to see whether a model is MoE / MTP-enabled BEFORE
#   downloading it: read the model card at ollama.com/library/<name>.
#   the description usually flags "Mixture of Experts" up front; `ollama
#   show` at the CLI hides that same information.
#
# IF YOU UPGRADE — M4 Pro 48 GB DELTA (rev. Sep 2026)
#   BANDWIDTH IS THE BIGGER HALF OF THIS, NOT THE RAM. base M3 ~100
#   GB/s -> M4 Pro 273 GB/s = 2.7x. every model already on this list
#   gets 2.7x faster on the same weights — gemma4:12b goes from its
#   MEASURED 11.0 tok/s here to ~30 tok/s there. that alone is the difference between "watchable" and
#   "usable", before a single new model enters the picture.
#
#   CAPACITY ROUGHLY DOUBLES. macOS hands the GPU ~75% of unified
#   memory, so 48 GB yields ~36 GB addressable against the ~18 GB this
#   machine actually gets (measured, see MEMORY BUDGET above). NOTE: an
#   earlier version of this note claimed the split jumps from ~2/3 to
#   ~75% at a 36 GB threshold, and that 48 GB therefore gains MORE than
#   2x. THAT WAS WRONG — the 24 GB machine already gets ~74%. the gain
#   is a clean 2x, which is still enough to open the entire 32B band
#   that is closed on 24 GB:
#     qwen3-coder:30b    18.6 GB  MoE, 3.3B active -> ~55-70 tok/s by
#                                 the corrected formula (3.4-4.4 GB
#                                 effective). the old 60-80 here was
#                                 only optimistic at the very top. THE
#                                 BEST LOCAL CODING SETUP short of a
#                                 Mac Studio. runs FASTER on M4 Pro
#                                 than qwen2.5-coder:14b does on the
#                                 M3 while being 2x the size — MoE
#                                 decouples speed from total size
#     qwen2.5-coder:32b  19.9 GB  flagship of that family, ~12 tok/s
#     qwen3:32b          20.2 GB  general chat / reasoning
#     qwq:32b            19.9 GB  reasoning specialist
#     gemma4:26b         18.6 GB  Google Gemma-4-26B-A4B-It. MoE, 26B
#                                 TOTAL / 4B ACTIVE (128 experts, 8 used
#                                 per token), 256K trained context,
#                                 Apache 2.0. package = 15.8 GB Q4_K_M
#                                 weights + 1.2 GB vision projector +
#                                 420M MTP draft head. ollama runs it
#                                 with speculative decoding by default
#                                 (draft-mtp, n_max=3), no extra config
#                                 needed — draft acceptance runs 60-78%.
#                                 MEASURED on M4 Pro 48 GB: 50-63 tok/s
#                                 eval, ~60 typical on later runs
#                                 (an earlier note said 55), drops to ~44 tok/s
#                                 at 7-8K prompt tokens as acceptance
#                                 falls; prompt processing ~600 tok/s.
#                                 CURRENT PICK for US-domestic in this
#                                 band — 4B active + MTP gives 26B-
#                                 quality output at close to 4B-model
#                                 speeds.
#                                 https://ollama.com/library/gemma4
#     gemma3:27b         17.4 GB  Google, dense predecessor. ~10 tok/s
