# WHAT ARE MoE AND MTP? — THE ACRONYMS BEHIND THE SPEEDUPS
# --------------------------------------------------------
# both MoE and MTP are architectural tricks that DECOUPLE "how big is
# the model?" from "how fast does it generate?". they solve different
# problems and can be combined (gemma4:26b uses both). understanding
# the difference matters when shopping for a model — a 30B dense and a
# 30B MoE take the same disk footprint but generate at very different
# speeds.
#
# MoE — MIXTURE OF EXPERTS
#   The problem it solves: a dense model reads every parameter for
#   every token. a 26B dense model reads 26B params per token — that's
#   why speed scales with model size.
#
#   The trick: replace the feed-forward layer (the fattest part of a
#   transformer block) with N parallel "expert" feed-forwards + a small
#   "router" that picks which K of them to fire for each token.
#
#   Concretely for gemma4:26b: 128 experts, router picks 8 per token.
#   all 128 must live in memory (any token might need any expert), but
#   only 8 actually do work per token. RESULT: 26B total parameters,
#   but only ~4B are ACTIVE per token. model is 26B on disk, generates
#   at ~4B-model speeds.
#
#   Analogy: a hospital with 128 specialists on staff. for each patient
#   (token) the triage desk (router) picks the 8 most relevant. you
#   still need all 128 employed and in the building — but only 8 do
#   work on each patient.
#
#   TRADEOFFS:
#     + much faster than a dense model of the same total size
#     + higher quality than a dense model of the same active size —
#       the extra capacity helps
#     - RAM budget is set by TOTAL size, not active size (all experts
#       have to fit in memory)
#     - routing can be uneven; some experts get more training signal
#       than others
#
# MTP — MULTI-TOKEN PREDICTION
#   The problem it solves: even with MoE, you're doing one forward pass
#   per token. generation is fundamentally sequential — token N has to
#   finish before token N+1 can start.
#
#   The trick: train a small "draft head" (a few extra layers, ~400M
#   params for gemma4:26b) whose job is to predict the NEXT FEW TOKENS
#   in parallel. at inference:
#     1. draft head proposes N tokens in one fast pass
#     2. main model verifies all N in ONE forward pass ("would I have
#        produced these?")
#     3. accepted tokens are kept, rejected ones trigger a fallback
#
#   Concretely for gemma4:26b: n_max=3, so draft head predicts 3 tokens
#   ahead. main model agrees with all 3 about 65% of the time; when it
#   does, you got 3 tokens for the cost of 2 forward passes instead of
#   3. partial matches still save something.
#
#   Analogy: you're dictating a letter. your assistant finishes your
#   sentences before you do. you proofread — if they nailed it, time
#   saved; if wrong, you correct and move on. same output either way.
#
#   KEY PROPERTY: output is EXACTLY IDENTICAL to non-MTP inference.
#   it's provably lossless — verified tokens are exactly what the main
#   model would have produced on its own. this is NOT a quality
#   tradeoff, only a speed one.
#
#   TRADEOFFS:
#     + 1.5-3x throughput at zero quality cost
#     + especially valuable on Apple Silicon where generation is
#       bandwidth-bound
#     - requires a trained draft head; you can't retrofit MTP onto a
#       model that wasn't trained with one
#     - draft acceptance drops on unusual text (creative writing,
#       unfamiliar code) — less speedup where the draft is less
#       predictive
#
# COMBINING THEM (gemma4:26b, MEASURED ~60 tok/s on M4 Pro)
#   a pure dense 26B on the same hardware would give ~13 tok/s, so the
#   two mechanisms together are worth ~4.6x. the split between them is
#   NOT cleanly separable from one end-to-end number, but roughly:
#     dense 26B baseline            ~13 tok/s
#     + MoE (4B active of 25.2B)    ~38 tok/s    x2.9
#     + MTP (60-78% acceptance)     ~60 tok/s    x1.6
#   MoE IS THE BIGGER HALF. CAVEAT: the MoE correction factor used
#   here was itself fitted to this model's measurement, so using it to
#   explain that same measurement is partly circular. the honest way
#   to split them is to disable speculative decoding and re-measure —
#   the difference IS the MTP contribution, exactly.
#
# WHEN SHOPPING FOR A MODEL: MECHANISM COUNT IS THE WRONG SCREEN
#   the intuitive rule — "prefer models with BOTH MoE and MTP" — is
#   wrong, and this section used to imply it. two reasons.
#
#   FIRST, IT SCREENS DOWN TO ONE MODEL. gemma4:26b is the only
#   both-and on ollama (table below). a filter returning a single
#   candidate isn't a filter.
#
#   SECOND, AND WORSE, IT PICKS THE WRONG MODELS. what sets generation
#   speed is BYTES READ PER TOKEN, full stop. MoE and MTP are two ways
#   to lower that number, not goals in themselves:
#     effective_GB ≈ total_GB × (active/total) × 1.7-2.2
#     tok/s        ≈ (bandwidth × 0.89) / effective_GB, ÷ MTP factor
#
#   THE COUNTEREXAMPLE, both ends measured:
#     gpt-oss:20b   MoE only, NO MTP.  reads 4.01 GB/token
#     gemma4:26b    MoE + MTP.         reads 6.48 GB/token
#   gpt-oss starts 1.6x ahead on bytes moved; gemma4:26b's MTP gives
#   back ~1.6x. THEY CANCEL — both land at ~60 tok/s on M4 Pro. the
#   model with ONE mechanism ties the model with TWO.
#
#   SO ASK, IN THIS ORDER:
#     1. total size — does it FIT? nothing else matters if it doesn't
#     2. active/total — this sets speed. a smaller, sparser model
#        beats a bigger, denser one even when the bigger one has more
#        tricks
#     3. does it ship an MTP head? — a real bonus, and PROVABLY
#        LOSSLESS (output is identical to non-MTP inference), so take
#        it when it's there. but it is a TIEBREAKER, NOT A GATE
#
#   ONE ASYMMETRY WORTH KNOWING: MTP is the part that DECAYS. draft
#   acceptance falls on unfamiliar text and long context — gemma4:26b
#   drops from ~60 to ~44 tok/s by 7-8K prompt tokens. a MoE-only
#   model has nothing to lose and holds its rate flat. FOR LONG
#   SESSIONS THE NO-MTP MODEL CAN END UP FASTER IN PRACTICE.
#
#   PRACTICAL UPSHOT ON THIS HARDWARE:
#     M3 24 GB -> gpt-oss:20b. MEASURED 22.2 tok/s and it definitely
#                 fits; gemma4:26b is still an untested prediction here
#     M4 Pro   -> gemma4:26b. same ~60 tok/s as gpt-oss:20b but a more
#                 capable model (26B vs 20B) and multimodal. the case
#                 is CAPABILITY AT SPEED PARITY, not mechanism count
#   both are US-domestic (Google, OpenAI), so the filter doesn't
#   separate them.
#
# MoE + MTP CROSS-REFERENCE (rev. Sep 2026)
#
#   MODEL                        MoE  MTP  NOTE
#   gemma4:26b                    Y    Y   the only both-and on ollama
#   gpt-oss:20b                   Y    N
#   gpt-oss:120b                  Y    N
#   qwen3:30b-a3b                 Y    N
#   qwen3-coder:30b               Y    N
#   qwen3:235b-a22b               Y    N
#   mixtral:8x7b / :8x22b         Y    N
#   deepseek-coder-v2:16b         Y    N
#   granite4:small-h              Y    N
#   llama4:scout / :maverick      Y    N
#   deepseek-v3                   Y    ?   MTP is architectural in
#                                          DeepSeek V3 upward, but
#                                          ollama quants don't always
#                                          bundle the draft head
#
#   gemma4:26b's MTP + 4B-active MoE is where the ~60 tok/s on M4 Pro
#   comes from — a pure dense 26B would give ~13. no other current
#   ollama model stacks both. DO NOT TURN THAT INTO A SCREENING RULE:
#   gpt-oss:20b is MoE-only and TIES IT at ~60 on the same hardware.
#   see the shopping section above.
#
#   gpt-oss:20b IS THE MORE USEFUL CALIBRATION POINT of the two,
#   precisely BECAUSE it has no MTP: its measured 22.2 tok/s on M3
#   pins the MoE correction at x1.69 with nothing to disentangle. the
#   x2.20 derived from gemma4:26b inherits whatever error is in the
#   assumed MTP factor, so trust it less. if you ever run gemma4:26b
#   with speculative decoding disabled, that single number would fix
#   both constants for good.
#
#   TO CHECK MTP ON ANY MODEL: `ollama show <model>` — if you see
#   `draft_num_predict` in the Parameters block, an MTP head is bundled
#   and active. absent = no MTP.


# MoE MODELS ON OLLAMA — LANDSCAPE (rev. Sep 2026)
# ---------------------------------------------------
# MoE trades TOTAL parameters for smaller ACTIVE-set-per-token: only a
# subset of expert FFNs fires per token, so throughput scales with the
# active count, not the total. this is why a 26B MoE with 4B active
# runs at close to 4B-model speeds.
#
# RULE OF THUMB ON APPLE SILICON — CORRECTED Sep 2026:
#   effective_GB ≈ total_GB × (active_params / total_params) × 1.7-2.2
#   tok/s        ≈ (bandwidth × 0.89) / effective_GB
#
# the correction factor is the important part and it was MISSING here
# before. the old form — bandwidth / (active_params × BPW / 8) ×
# 0.7-0.8 — treats the routed experts as the only thing read per token.
# they aren't: attention, embeddings, the router and any shared expert
# are DENSE and get read on every single token.
#
# THE TWO MEASUREMENTS IT IS FITTED TO:
#   gpt-oss:20b on M3 (13.8 GB, 3.6B active of 20.9B)
#     old formula: 3.6B × 4 bits / 8 = 1.8 GB    → ~42-50 tok/s
#     MEASURED                                     22.2 tok/s
#     implied bytes/token 89 ÷ 22.2 = 4.01 GB, vs a naive
#     13.8 × (3.6/20.9) = 2.38 GB  →  ×1.69
#   gemma4:26b on M4 Pro (18.6 GB, 4B active of 25.2B)
#     MEASURED ~60 tok/s typical; back out its ~1.6× MTP head → 37.5
#     implied 243 ÷ 37.5 = 6.48 GB, vs a naive 2.95 GB  →  ×2.20
#   NOTE THE ASYMMETRY IN HOW MUCH TO TRUST THESE. the gpt-oss point is
#   CLEAN — no MTP, so nothing to disentangle. the gemma4 point is
#   derived, because the ~1.6× MTP factor is itself inferred from the
#   same measurement. prefer ×1.69 when you have to pick one.
#
# SO IT IS A RANGE, NOT A CONSTANT — two points, spanning 1.69 to 2.20,
# and no reason to think either endpoint is universal. gemma4:26b sits
# high plausibly because it carries a shared always-on expert and a
# vision projector on top of the routed experts. use ×2.2 for a
# conservative estimate, ×1.7 for an optimistic one, and treat any MoE
# prediction here as +/-25% until it has actually been run.
#
# the download size below is the total DISK footprint — it determines
# whether weights FIT, not how fast they RUN. active-param count is
# what sets throughput. cross-cutting reference; per-hardware picks are
# in the sections above.
#
# tables use [US] to mark US-domestic origins (see also the US-domestic
# filter discussion above, and the subset callout at the end of this
# section).
#
# FITS ON 24 GB UNIFIED (weights <= ~14 GB comfortable)
#   gpt-oss:20b       [US] 13.8 GB   OpenAI. 20B / ~3.6B active. no MTP.
#                                    ceiling model on 24 GB, trivial on 48
#   qwen3:30b-a3b          18.6 GB   Alibaba. 30B / 3B active. tight fit
#                                    on 24 GB (sysctl required); no MTP
#   qwen3-coder:30b        18.6 GB   coding variant, same MoE shape.
#                                    best local coding on 48 GB
#   deepseek-coder-v2:16b   8.9 GB   16B / 2.4B active, 2024 vintage but
#                                    still relevant as a small coder
#
# FITS ON 48 GB (weights <= ~34 GB with headroom)
#   gemma4:26b        [US] 18.6 GB   Google. 26B / 4B active + MTP head.
#                                    the standout on this hardware; runs
#                                    at 50-63 tok/s with speculative
#                                    decoding. https://ollama.com/library/gemma4
#   granite4:small-h  [US] 19.5 GB   IBM Granite 4 hybrid MoE, Apache 2.0
#   mixtral:8x7b             26 GB   Mistral, 47B / 12.9B active. the
#                                    original open MoE benchmark; per-
#                                    token slower than newer small-active
#                                    MoEs because active count is bigger
#
# MAC STUDIO / SERVER TERRITORY (weights >= 48 GB)
#   gpt-oss:120b      [US] 65.4 GB   OpenAI. 120B / ~5B active — fast
#                                    per-token despite total size
#   mixtral:8x22b           ~80 GB   Mistral. 141B / 39B active
#   llama4:scout      [US]  ~65 GB   Meta. 109B / 17B active
#   qwen3:235b-a22b        ~140 GB   Alibaba. 235B / 22B active
#   llama4:maverick   [US] ~245 GB   Meta. 400B / 17B active
#   deepseek-v3            ~350 GB   671B / 37B active. current Sonnet-
#                                    class open-weights target — see the
#                                    Sonnet tier discussion below
#
# US-DOMESTIC SUBSET — WHAT THE FILTER LEAVES YOU WITH
#   6 models total across all tiers: gpt-oss (20b, 120b) from OpenAI,
#   gemma4:26b from Google, granite4:small-h from IBM, llama4 (scout,
#   maverick) from Meta. gemma4:26b is the only one with an MTP head.
#
#   THE HONEST GAP: there is no US-domestic MoE code specialist.
#   qwen3-coder:30b is the killer local coding setup on 48 GB and it
#   is Alibaba — same conclusion the dense US-DOMESTIC FILTER section
#   reaches, MoE has not fixed it. US-domestic substitutes for coding:
#     small:  granite4 or gemma4:12b (dense), a real step down
#     medium: gemma4:26b — general model that codes adequately, not a
#             coding specialist. best available under the filter
#     large:  llama4:scout / :maverick, still weaker on code than
#             qwen3-coder or deepseek-coder at similar tiers
#
#   BIG-RIG TIER: llama4:maverick (400B / 17B active) is the US-
#   domestic answer to deepseek-v3 (671B / 37B active). Maverick is
#   smaller and faster per token because the active count is smaller;
#   DeepSeek still wins on benchmarks. if you are spending Mac Studio
#   Ultra money for Sonnet-class capability, this is where the filter
#   costs the most.
#
# HOW TO SPOT NEW ONES: ollama.com/library sorted by size doesn't filter
# for MoE, but the model description on each page usually flags "Mixture
# of Experts" up front, often with the active-parameter count. the CLI
# `ollama show` does NOT surface expert_count / expert_used_count — so
# the web page is more reliable than the CLI for shopping. this list is
# frozen at Aug 2026; the DeepSeek / Kimi / MiniMax / Qwen release
# cadence means it goes stale fast. re-check the library before you
# invest in a new pick.


# HOW THESE COMPARE TO ANTHROPIC'S FRONTIER MODELS (HAIKU, SONNET, OPUS)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TL;DR: the gap is large, but the small models can substitute for
# narrow, well-defined tasks if you set expectations right.
#
# the weight class reality: frontier models (Haiku 4.5, Sonnet 4.6,
# Opus 4.7) are estimated at hundreds of billions of parameters and
# trained on trillions of tokens. a 7-8B local is roughly 1-3% the
# parameter count of frontier-class. even HAIKU 4.5 — Anthropic's
# smallest current model — substantially outperforms any 7-8B local on
# MMLU-Pro, GPQA, HumanEval, MATH, etc. Sonnet and Opus are another
# tier above that.
#
# WHERE LOCAL 7-8B IS "GOOD ENOUGH" TO ACTUALLY USE
#   - single-turn factual Q&A on common topics
#   - bash / python / SQL one-liners and small functions
#   - boilerplate (CRUD endpoints, config files, regex)
#   - summarization of short documents
#   - format conversion (JSON <-> YAML, markdown tables)
#   - tab-completion-style code assist in an editor (human reviews each)
#
# WHERE THE GAP BITES YOU
#   - multi-step reasoning that requires holding a plan together
#   - reading a large codebase and proposing cross-file changes
#   - tool use / agentic workflows — 7-8Bs are unreliable callers
#   - long-context retrieval beyond ~16k tokens (recall degrades fast)
#   - hard math / proofs / olympiad problems
#   - subtle instruction following (negative constraints, output schemas)
#   - refusal calibration and safety
#
# PER-MODEL TAKE
#
#   qwen2.5:7b
#     analog:    a noticeably weaker Haiku for general chat
#     pitch:     best general-purpose pick in the 7B class. strong
#                reasoning-for-size, Apache 2.0, 32k-128k context (recall
#                drops well before that). still well behind Haiku 4.5 on
#                multi-step tasks.
#
#   llama3.1:8b
#     analog:    similar tier to qwen2.5:7b, slightly weaker overall but
#                better tool-use formatting
#     pitch:     Meta's broad-coverage workhorse. native 128k context
#                (recall shaky past ~16k). good for chat and function-
#                calling experimentation. behind Qwen on math/code.
#
#   qwen2.5-coder:7b
#     analog:    a weaker Haiku on code, a much weaker Sonnet
#     pitch:     the standout small code model. on HumanEval-style
#                problems it gets surprisingly close to frontier (mid-
#                high 80s % vs ~95% for Sonnet 4.6). falls apart on
#                multi-file edits, real-world refactors, ambiguous
#                specs. genuinely useful as an in-editor completion
#                engine.
#
#   codellama:7b
#     analog:    a weaker Haiku from 2023
#     pitch:     SUPERSEDED. released Aug 2023, weaker than every model
#                above, no instruction-following advantage. keep only if
#                you specifically need its `code-infill` mode. otherwise
#                replace with qwen2.5-coder:7b.
#
# CONCRETE RULE OF THUMB
#   - tasks where Sonnet/Opus would think 10+ seconds and write 200+
#     lines: DON'T USE 7B LOCALS, they will get it wrong in subtle ways.
#   - tasks where Haiku would respond in 2 seconds with a short snippet:
#     7B LOCALS CAN OFTEN DO IT, with a higher error rate you'll need
#     to catch.
#   - privacy-sensitive workflows where you'd otherwise not be able to
#     send the data: THE QUESTION CHANGES — a 70%-as-good local beats
#     not using AI at all.
#
# the honest framing isn't "local vs frontier" — it's "local plus a
# human reviewer vs frontier with less review". for code completion
# in a tight feedback loop, a 7B model wins on latency and cost. for
# complex reasoning where you'd trust the output, frontier wins
# decisively.
#
# see also: [[anthropic-models]] for current Claude 4.x lineup and
#           when to pick each.


# WHAT SETUP IS REQUIRED FOR A LOCAL HAIKU-CLASS MODEL?
# -----------------------------------------------------
#
# HONEST UPFRONT: no local model currently matches Haiku 4.5 capability-
# for-capability. the closest open-weights contenders (Llama 3.3 70B,
# Qwen 2.5 72B, DeepSeek-V3) approach Haiku on some tasks but lag on
# others. "Haiku-class" is aspirational — and the hardware bill scales
# accordingly.
#
# QUALIFIER (checked Aug 2026): the "HONEST UPFRONT" line above was
# written about 70B DENSE models and is now TOO PESSIMISTIC ON CODE.
# large open-weights MoE coders changed the picture:
#
#   Claude Haiku 4.5            73.3%  SWE-bench Verified (Anthropic's
#                                      own number: avg of 50 trials, no
#                                      test-time compute, 128K thinking
#                                      budget, full 500-problem set)
#   Qwen3-Coder-480B-A35B       66.5%  Pass@1 (Qwen's own reporting,
#                                      Jul 2025). ~7 points back — a
#                                      real gap, but a NARROW one, not
#                                      the chasm the 7-8B framing above
#                                      describes
#
# and the gap kept closing through 2026. by Aug 2026 aggregators put
# several open-weights coders (DeepSeek V4 Pro, Kimi K3, MiniMax V4)
# at or near the proprietary frontier on SWE-bench Verified. treat the
# exact standings as volatile — they move monthly.
#
# READ BENCHMARK NUMBERS WITH CARE. HARNESS VARIANCE SWAMPS MODEL
# DIFFERENCES: vals.ai runs a minimal bash-tool-only agent and reports
# Claude Opus 5 at 97% / DeepSeek V4 Pro at 96.4%, while vendor-reported
# numbers for the same benchmark sit 20+ points lower. NEVER compare a
# score from one source against a score from another — only compare
# within a single harness. the two numbers in the table above are each
# vendor self-reported, so even THAT pairing is indicative, not rigorous.
#
# WHAT THE BENCHMARK STILL DOESN'T CAPTURE — and why "Haiku-class on
# SWE-bench" is not "Haiku-class in practice":
#   - tool-calling reliability across a long agent loop (Haiku holds a
#     50-turn session without malformed calls; open coders drift)
#   - instruction adherence on negative constraints + output schemas
#   - effective long-context recall, which degrades more gracefully on
#     the frontier models than the stated windows suggest
#   - calibration — knowing when to stop vs. confidently emitting
#     plausible-wrong code
#   - BREADTH: Qwen3-Coder is a CODE SPECIALIST. Haiku 4.5 is a general
#     model that codes well. outside code, they aren't comparable
#
# SO: for single-shot / few-shot code generation with a human reviewing
# output, large open-weights coders are genuinely Haiku-class as of
# 2026. for autonomous agentic coding — the workload that would justify
# the hardware — they remain behind. the hardware tiers below are
# unchanged; what changed is that Tier 3 now buys real code capability
# rather than an approximation. see [[ai-code-cli]].
#
# TIER 1 — "CLOSE, NOT EQUAL" (~$2-5K)
# the cheapest path to running a 70B-class model.
#   - 2x used RTX 3090 24 GB ($800-1000 each) -> 48 GB VRAM via model
#     split. or 1x RTX 4090/5090 (24/32 GB) + 64 GB system RAM for
#     overflow.
#   - runs Llama 3.3 70B or Qwen 2.5 72B at Q4_K_M (~40 GB weights),
#     ~10-20 tok/s.
#   - hits maybe 60-80% of Haiku on general tasks; weaker on long-
#     context, tool use, multi-step reasoning.
#   - ~400-600 W under load, audible fans, real heat. needs a desktop
#     case.
#
# TIER 2 — "COMFORTABLE 70B" (~$5-10K)
# single big card, 70B at high quant with long context.
#   - NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada 48 GB (~$6800 new, $4-5K used), or A6000 48
#     GB (used $3-4K).
#   - or NVIDIA DGX Spark (formerly Project DIGITS, ~$3K, 128 GB unified
#     memory, released 2025) — designed exactly for this; runs 70B
#     comfortably, slower than a discrete GPU but much less hassle.
#   - or Mac Studio M3 Max 96-128 GB ($4-6K) — slower but quiet, low-
#     power, plug-and-play.
#   - Llama 3.3 70B at Q6/Q8 with 32k+ context, ~20-30 tok/s on the
#     6000 Ada.
#   - 80-90% of Haiku on general tasks; still trails on tool use and
#     complex agentic flows.
#
# TIER 3 — "ACTUALLY FRONTIER-ISH LOCALLY" (~$7-15K)
# realistic path to running a true frontier-tier MoE model at home.
#   - Mac Studio M3 Ultra 256-512 GB unified memory ($7-10K) — runs
#     DeepSeek-V3 671B at Q4 (~350 GB on disk, ~370 GB resident) at
#     5-15 tok/s. cheapest way to have a genuinely frontier-class model
#     locally; slow but it's THERE.
#   - alternative: 2x DGX Spark in cluster (~$6K, 256 GB combined) —
#     same idea, all-NVIDIA stack.
#   - first tier where you can credibly say "I'm running a Haiku-or-
#     better model at home". caveat: prompt-processing speed on Apple
#     Silicon is much slower than NVIDIA, so first-token latency feels
#     sluggish on long inputs.
#
# TIER 4 — "DATACENTER AT HOME" ($30K+)
#   - 4-8x H100 80 GB, or 2x AMD MI300X 192 GB, or a refurb DGX H100.
#   - runs Llama 3.1 405B at high precision, DeepSeek-V3 at FP8,
#     frontier-tier reasoning models at usable speed.
#   - where you genuinely match frontier inference quality and speed.
#   - power: 3-10 kW. you're paying for a dedicated 240V circuit.
#
# THE COST REALITY CHECK
# Haiku 4.5 via Anthropic API runs roughly $1/MTok in, $5/MTok out.
# heavy personal use ≈ ~$30-150/month. even Tier 1 hardware at $3K
# takes 2-7 years to amortize against API spend, BEFORE electricity.
# the case for local ISN'T COST — it's:
#   1. privacy — code / docs / conversations never leave your network
#   2. offline / air-gapped use (labs, secure envs, planes)
#   3. no rate limits or content-policy refusals
#   4. stable behavior — your model doesn't change underneath you
#   5. fine-tuning — adapt the weights to your data
# if none of those apply, the API is the rational choice and it isn't
# close.
#
# THE PRAGMATIC STEP-UP FROM AN 8 GB LAPTOP
# cheapest serious upgrade: a desktop with 1x RTX 5090 32 GB or a used
# 3090 + 64 GB RAM for ~$2-3K. that puts you in Tier 1 and is the
# highest jump-per-dollar you'll see. going to Tier 2/3 is a bigger
# commitment of money and physical space, and the gains over Tier 1
# are meaningful but not enormous unless you specifically need long
# context or MoE-class reasoning.


# WHAT SETUP IS REQUIRED FOR A LOCAL SONNET-CLASS MODEL?
# ------------------------------------------------------
#
# HONEST UPFRONT: matching Sonnet-class locally is meaningfully harder
# than matching Haiku-class. no current open-weights model genuinely
# matches Sonnet 4.6. closest contenders: DeepSeek-V3 671B (MoE, 37B
# active), DeepSeek-R1, Llama 3.1 405B, Llama 4 Maverick (400B MoE).
# they approach Sonnet on benchmarks but lag on real-world tool use,
# multi-step refactoring, and effective long-context recall. so the
# hardware bill below buys you "Sonnet-ish on most tasks", not "Sonnet
# equivalent".
#
# the Haiku -> Sonnet jump in open-weights land roughly means going
# from a 70B dense to a 400B+ MoE. memory requirements 5-10x from there.
#
# TIER A — "SONNET-ISH VIA MOE" (~$10-15K)
# the cheapest entry point.
#   - Mac Studio M3 Ultra 512 GB unified memory (~$10K, released 2025) —
#     runs DeepSeek-V3 671B at Q4 (~370 GB resident), Llama 3.1 405B at
#     Q6-Q8, or Llama 4 Maverick at Q8.
#   - ~5-15 tok/s generation; prompt processing is the bottleneck (long
#     inputs feel sluggish — 30-60 s to first token on 32k context).
#   - hits ~75-85% of Sonnet on general tasks; closer on raw code,
#     further on agentic / tool-heavy workflows.
#   - THE ONLY "single appliance" path to Sonnet-class locally. anything
#     else gets complicated fast.
#
# TIER B — "COMFORTABLE SONNET-CLASS ON NVIDIA" ($20-50K)
#   - 4x RTX 6000 Ada 48 GB (~$28K) -> 192 GB VRAM. runs Llama 3.1 405B
#     at Q4 cleanly with usable context, or DeepSeek-V3 at heavy quant
#     (Q2/Q3 — quality drops).
#   - or 2x H100 80 GB ($40-50K) -> 160 GB HBM3. same envelope, much
#     faster.
#   - or 2x AMD MI300X 192 GB ($30-50K) -> 384 GB HBM3. best memory
#     headroom in this tier; ROCm tooling is the catch.
#   - ~30-60 tok/s on 405B Q4, 80-150 tok/s on smaller models. fast
#     prompt processing — long-context feels frontier-like.
#   - power: 1.5-3 kW. real desktop / open-frame rig, dedicated 20A or
#     30A circuit, real cooling.
#
# TIER C — "PROPER INFERENCE RIG" ($50-150K)
#   - 4-8x H100 80 GB ($50-120K) — the canonical setup. runs DeepSeek-V3
#     at Q8/FP8, Llama 3.1 405B at full BF16, high-throughput batched
#     serving (can host for a team).
#   - or 4x MI300X ($60-100K) — 768 GB HBM3, runs everything above at
#     higher precision.
#   - or 2x NVIDIA B200 / Blackwell (when supply normalizes) — same
#     envelope, lower power.
#   - 100-300+ tok/s, 8k-32k context at full quality, can fine-tune.
#   - 5-10 kW, 240V dedicated circuit, real noise (server-grade fans).
#
# TIER D — "DATACENTER AT HOME / SMALL TEAM" ($150K+)
#   - DGX H100 / DGX H200 node ($300K+), or 8x MI300X.
#   - frontier MoE at FP8/FP16 with full context, fine-tuning, can
#     serve a small org.
#   - at this point seriously consider whether RENTING from Lambda /
#     Modal / RunPod isn't 10x cheaper for hours-per-day usage.
#
# THE COST REALITY CHECK
# Sonnet 4.6 via Anthropic API is roughly $3/MTok in, $15/MTok out.
# heavy personal use ≈ $200-500/month; small team easily $2-5K/month.
#   - Tier A ($10K)  breakeven ≈ 2-4 years vs heavy personal API spend.
#   - Tier B ($30K)  breakeven ≈ 6-18 months vs team-scale API spend.
#   - Tier C ($100K) only sane if serving multiple users continuously,
#                    or hard data-residency / air-gap requirements.
# cost is essentially never the winning argument until you're serving
# a team or running 24/7. the case for local is the same as Haiku-class
# (privacy / offline / no rate limits / stable weights / fine-tuning),
# just at higher stakes.
#
# THE PRAGMATIC ANSWER
#   - want to TRY Sonnet-class locally without committing real money:
#     Mac Studio M3 Ultra 512 GB at ~$10K is the only sane consumer
#     entry. slower than NVIDIA, but it actually fits the weights
#     without complicated rigging; resale value holds well if you bail.
#   - real team-scale use case: 2x H100 80 GB is the proven sweet spot —
#     frontier-class throughput on 405B, room for fine-tuning,
#     manageable in a colo rack.
#   - solo: STOP HERE. Tier B+ for one person is almost never the right
#     call vs paying Anthropic by the token.
#
# the consumer hardware curve is bending toward this getting much
# cheaper over the next 18-24 months. M4 Ultra Mac Studios, NVIDIA
# DGX Spark cluster configs, AMD Strix Halo (128 GB unified APU, 2025)
# are all aimed at this market. if your need isn't urgent, waiting a
# year halves the price.


# WHAT SETUP IS REQUIRED FOR A LOCAL OPUS-CLASS MODEL?
# ----------------------------------------------------
#
# HONEST UPFRONT: a true Opus 4.7 equivalent does not currently exist
# in open weights, period. the Sonnet question had close-but-not-quite
# contenders (DeepSeek-V3, Llama 3.1 405B, Llama 4 Maverick). the Opus
# question doesn't really have any — the gap from Sonnet to Opus in
# agentic reasoning, long-horizon planning, hardest-tier coding,
# creative judgment, and refusal calibration is exactly where open-
# weights are weakest. so what the hardware below buys you is "the
# best open-weight model at full precision with frontier throughput",
# which is approximately Sonnet+ on benchmarks and NOTICEABLY BELOW
# Opus on the workflows that justify reaching for Opus in the first
# place.
#
# closest contenders to aim for: DeepSeek-R1 671B at full precision
# with extended thinking, DeepSeek-V3 671B at FP8/BF16, Llama 3.1 405B
# at BF16, Llama 4 Behemoth (~2T total MoE, if/when it ships).
#
# the Sonnet -> Opus jump is less about another 10x memory and more
# about needing FRONTIER-TIER THROUGHPUT so extended-thinking and
# agentic workloads aren't punitively slow. that moves you from
# "consumer-adjacent" into datacenter-grade infrastructure.
#
# TIER I — "THE HONEST 'APPROACHING OPUS' FLOOR" (~$15-30K)
# same hardware as Sonnet Tier A, running the same models — there is no
# $15K leap to Opus-class.
#   - Mac Studio M3 Ultra 512 GB (~$10-12K) — runs DeepSeek-V3/R1 at
#     Q4-Q6, 5-15 tok/s.
#   - or DGX Spark cluster (4x at ~$12K, 512 GB combined) — same
#     envelope, all-NVIDIA stack.
#   - hits ~70-85% of Opus on simple chat/code, 30-50% on hard agentic
#     / multi-step / long-context tasks. the Opus-tier workflows are
#     EXACTLY the ones this tier can't keep up with.
#   - if your goal is "Opus-class at home for solo use", this tier is
#     the MOST you should spend — and it still isn't Opus.
#
# TIER II — "REAL FRONTIER HARDWARE" ($100-300K)
# where you start running frontier open-weight models at full precision
# with usable throughput.
#   - 8x NVIDIA H100 80 GB ($120-150K) -> 640 GB HBM3. runs DeepSeek-
#     V3/R1 at FP8 with full context, fine-tuning, batch-serves a team.
#   - or 8x H200 141 GB ($200K+) -> 1.1 TB HBM3e. headroom for Llama 4
#     Behemoth or future 1T+ models.
#   - or 4x NVIDIA B200 192 GB (when supply normalizes) -> 768 GB
#     HBM3e, lower power per FLOP.
#   - or 8x AMD MI300X 192 GB ($150-250K) -> 1.5 TB HBM3. biggest
#     memory pool in this tier; ROCm tooling is the catch.
#   - 100-300+ tok/s on 405B/V3, 8k-32k context at full quality,
#     fine-tuning on the table.
#   - 10-15 kW. server room, real cooling, 240V industrial, ~$500-1000
#     per month electricity alone.
#   - STILL NOT Opus-equivalent on the hardest workflows, but it's the
#     closest you can buy.
#
# TIER III — "GENUINE FRONTIER INFRASTRUCTURE" ($500K-3M+)
#   - multiple DGX H100/H200 nodes, or NVIDIA GB200 NVL72 rack (~$3M
#     for a full config), or B200/B300 clusters.
#   - hyperscaler/lab infrastructure. can run models bigger than what's
#     publicly released, fine-tune frontier-scale, serve hundreds of
#     users.
#   - at this scale YOU SHOULD NOT BE BUYING. rent equivalent capacity
#     from CoreWeave / Lambda / Crusoe / a colo provider on demand —
#     order of magnitude cheaper for hours-per-day usage and you don't
#     deal with depreciation as H200 -> B200 -> Rubin keeps compressing.
#
# THE COST REALITY CHECK (sharper here than Sonnet)
# Opus 4.7 via Anthropic API is roughly $15/MTok in, $75/MTok out.
# heavy personal use ≈ $500-2000/month; team workloads $5-20K/month.
#   - Tier I  ($15K)  breakeven ≈ 1-3 years vs heavy personal Opus
#                     spend — BUT IT'S NOT RUNNING OPUS-CLASS quality,
#                     so this isn't really a substitution.
#   - Tier II ($150K) breakeven ≈ 1-2 years vs sustained team Opus
#                     spend, ignoring electricity/colo/staff time.
#                     interesting at small-org scale with privacy reqs.
#   - Tier III ($1M+) only makes sense if you're a lab/startup doing
#                     fine-tuning research, or serving 24/7 prod.
# the case for local at Opus-tier is NARROWER than at lower tiers:
# privacy, air-gapped requirements, hard data residency, very heavy
# fine-tuning. for everything else, the API is so obviously the
# rational choice that the hardware conversation is almost academic.
#
# THE PRAGMATIC ANSWER
#   - DON'T try to match Opus-class locally as an individual. you
#     can't, and the closest attempt (Tier I) doesn't actually
#     substitute for the workflows you'd want Opus for.
#   - real privacy-constrained team use case: plan for Tier II in
#     12-18 months. hardware is depreciating fast (Blackwell B200
#     deliveries in 2025 dropped used H100 prices ~30%; AMD MI325X /
#     MI350 series in 2025-2026 keep compressing prices). waiting is
#     correct unless you're paying $20K+/month on Opus API today.
#   - WATCH-FOR SIGNAL: if Qwen 4, DeepSeek-V4, Llama 5, or some
#     yet-unannounced open-weights release in 2026 genuinely matches
#     Opus on agentic benchmarks, the Tier I/II conversation changes
#     overnight. until then, Opus-class workloads -> Anthropic API
#     is correct for ~99% of users.
#
# HONEST SUMMARY OF THE FULL LADDER
#   Haiku-class    reachable for $3-10K (Tier 1-2 above)
#   Sonnet-class   approachable for $10-50K with caveats (Tier A-B)
#   Opus-class     NOT REACHABLE at home today at any consumer-feasible
#                  budget. the hardware that gets close is also the
#                  hardware you'd rent rather than own.


# WHAT SETUP IS REQUIRED FOR A LOCAL MYTHOS-CLASS (OR FABLE-CLASS) MODEL?
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# context: Anthropic's published model ordering, top-down, is
# Mythos -> Fable -> Opus -> Sonnet -> Haiku. so "Mythos-class" sits two
# tiers ABOVE Opus, "Fable-class" one tier above.
#
# HONEST ANSWER: a local Mythos-class equivalent is NOT ACHIEVABLE in
# 2026, period. the Opus section above already landed at "not really
# reachable at any consumer-feasible budget". Mythos sits above that,
# and the proprietary-frontier vs open-weights gap has been WIDENING,
# not closing, through 2025-2026. so the situation is WORSE than
# Opus-class, not just incrementally harder.
#
# TIER α — "HONEST FLOOR — THERE IS NO FLOOR"
# no budget makes this work. there is currently NO open-weights model
# in the above-Opus tier. DeepSeek-V3/R1, Llama 4 Behemoth, Qwen 3 are
# all ~Sonnet-tier on benchmarks and below Opus on real workflows.
# spending $30K on Mac Studio Ultra clusters or $300K on H200s buys
# you the same Sonnet+ models you'd run for Sonnet-class workloads,
# NOT anything Mythos-equivalent. you can't buy hardware out of this
# problem — the models you'd need don't exist in open weights.
#
# TIER β — "BEST PLAUSIBLE 2026 OPEN-WEIGHTS AT FRONTIER HARDWARE"
#                                                          ($300K-1M)
# if a 2026 open-weights release (Llama 5, DeepSeek-V4, Qwen 4 Max,
# future GPT-OSS, or something unannounced) genuinely matches Opus:
#   - 8x H200 141 GB (~$200K+) -> 1.1 TB HBM3e, runs ~2T-param dense
#     or MoE models at FP8.
#   - or 8x B200 192 GB when supply is real (~$250K+) -> 1.5 TB,
#     lower power-per-FLOP.
#   - or 8x AMD MI325X 256 GB (released late 2025) -> 2 TB HBM3e.
#   - 15-25 kW, colo or real server room.
#   - even with this hardware, you'd be running an Opus-class model,
#     NOT a Mythos-class one.
#
# TIER γ — "FRONTIER INFRASTRUCTURE / LAB-SCALE" ($2M-10M+)
#   - DGX GB200 NVL72 rack ($3M) or multiple H200 clusters.
#   - what Anthropic, OpenAI, Google DeepMind, Meta use to TRAIN
#     Mythos-class models in the first place.
#   - wouldn't let you replicate Mythos — you don't have the weights —
#     but it's the infrastructure tier required if a true above-Opus
#     model ever leaks or is open-sourced.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL PROBLEM
# even if hardware were free, two hard walls remain:
#   1. Mythos's weights AREN'T PUBLIC. given the cyber / national-
#      security framing around recent suspensions, extraordinarily
#      unlikely to be released openly ever.
#   2. the closest open-weights model is, at best, Opus-class by late
#      2026 (optimistically). the gap from Sonnet-class open-weights to
#      Opus-class took roughly a year (DeepSeek-V3 -> V4-class). above-
#      Opus open-weights is plausibly a 2027-2028 thing, not 2026.
#
# PRAGMATIC ANSWER
# there isn't one. if your use case genuinely requires Mythos-class
# capability and you can't get it via Anthropic's API:
#   - wait for upstream availability to resolve (most API restrictions
#     get unwound once technical evidence is shared).
#   - use Opus 4.7 in the meantime — on most workflows the Opus ->
#     Mythos delta is smaller than the Sonnet -> Opus delta.
#   - DON'T build a local rig hoping to substitute — the hardware
#     would be wasted because no model exists locally that does the
#     job. this is the same situation as 2023 with GPT-4: there was
#     no local substitute for nearly two years.
#
